A||||13||Monkeyman||TConv lockup in exactly 3.2 miles||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||01-27-2000||03:20 PM||||I work out of town and my tranny shifts fine (when it isn't low on fluid) but it always takes exactly 3.2 miles for the torque converter to lock up when I'm on my way to/from work.  This is an '86 auto/Iron Duke.  I figure that it takes that long before the tranny fluid gets warm enough to make everything work right?  Would changing my filter make any difference.  I don't want to put any more $$ into this as I'm almost ready to get my '85 GT and it definitely needs work.  I'd rather sink my cash into the GT, but I have to make sure the '86 gets me to/from work until spring when the GT is done.  Any suggestions?||||reg||5
Z||000001||DRH||01-27-2000||03:46 PM||||Monkey,<P>There are several criteria for the TCC to activate. Not sure if temp is one. Are you going 3.2 miles at a fairly constant speed in 3rd gear with no brake before it locks?<P>Anyway, I really don't think TCC lockup is required for safe operation (I'm sure someone will correct me if I'm wrong   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) The worst it should do is hurt your highway MPG <B>slightly</B>. If that's your only problem I would save the $$$s for the GT.<P>You mentioned low on fluid... If you have a leak, check the level religiously!!! Running low on fluid is a good way to kill a tranny!!!<P><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 01-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-27-2000||06:33 PM||||I don't believe tranny temp is a control variable under normal conditions. It could be the TC lockup valve sticks when cold. that would do it as would low fluid.<P>If that's all that's the problem you can probly get by for awhile.<P>If the TC wasn't locking at all I'd worry more. If the TC doesn't lock at all the tranny gets allot hotter than it normally would. That will hurt it. Also hurts gas milage considerably.||||reg||1
Z||000003||GREENDANO||01-27-2000||07:28 PM||||125 C transaxles will survive without the lock up convertor working.  Before '84 I believe they were all non'lock-up.  It's the transaxles with overdrive that will be short lived if the TC isn't locking up due to trans fluid getting too hot.  Not running with the TC locked does hurt your mileage, that's for sure, but a new solenoid, which is usually but not always the problem with lock up concerns would be a good place to start.  I replace a lot of them on Oldsmobiles all the time.  GM has revised the design many times. <BR>  The TC lock-up is controlled by the ECM. Your car must be getting up to the right temp at 3.2 miles so the ECM locks the TC.  My 85 GT used to go about 1 mile before lock-up.  But that was with an almost inevitable stop light and then start from a stop which would make the motor heat up all the sooner.  <BR>  Maybe you just have a weak T-stat that isn't letting the motor heat up as quickly, or maybe, as posted from another thread...  It's pretty cold where you are, and vehicles take longer to heat up to operating temp.  BRRR.  I love my remote start on my Blazer!!  -10F seats are mighty hard on the posterior side in the morning!<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||3
Z||000004||Monkeyman||01-28-2000||08:16 PM||||Thanx, everyone.  I guess I'll just save my $$ for the GT as long as it's not going to hurt anything.  I'm not particularly worried about fuel mileage.  I'm still getting around 20mpg.  I may invest in a new thermostat, though.  Any suggestions as to what temp I should get?||||reg||1
